WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.780
Setting up to bake for sale: weigh, test, and stay safe.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.720
This is the video for module 1 of Basic Baking and Snacks Production.

3
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:19.570
When you bake for sale, every bun must be the same. A cup of flour weighs differently every time, so use a digital scale that reads in 1 g steps.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.590
Put the empty bowl on the scale and press tare, so it shows zero.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.920
Add the ingredient slowly. Spoon out any extra. Do not pour it back into the bag with a wet spoon.

6
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.980
Tare again before the next one.

7
00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.700
For salt, nutmeg or yeast under 10 g, use measuring spoons levelled with a knife.

8
00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:50.950
Write the real weights on your batch sheet. Store flour and sugar sealed and off the floor, date-label opened packs, and use the oldest first.

9
00:00:52.200 --> 00:01:00.800
Raising agents lose strength with age, heat and damp, and a flat batch wastes flour, oil and gas. So test them the evening before.

10
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:06.410
Stir half a teaspoon of baking powder into hot water. It should fizz strongly at once.

11
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.680
Add a pinch of baking soda to vinegar or lemon juice. It should fizz immediately.

12
00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:24.640
Stir 1 teaspoon of yeast and 1 of sugar into about 60 ml of lukewarm water. After about 10 minutes you should see foam. No foam means the yeast is dead.

13
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:38.110
For example, Halima in Kaduna plans 4 batches of Nigerian buns, 2 of doughnuts and 2 of rock buns. Adding up the recipes, she needs 3,500 g of flour.

14
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:47.660
She adds 10% for dusting, spills and a test batch. That is 3,850 g, so she buys 4 kg.

15
00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:59.530
The recipes need 12 eggs, so she buys 15 in case one is cracked or bad. Crack each egg into a separate cup first, so one bad egg does not spoil the bowl.

16
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:07.780
She tests her raising agents before going to market, so she neither runs out nor leaves flour to attract weevils.

17
00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:17.310
Oven dials are a guide, not a promise. For example, Ngozi in Enugu puts an oven thermometer in the middle of the middle shelf.

18
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:27.900
She sets the dial to 180 and waits 20 minutes. The thermometer reads 160, so her oven runs about 20 degrees low.

19
00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:37.020
For rock buns at 200 degrees, she turns the dial higher and checks again until the thermometer reads close to 200.

20
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:49.860
Then she bakes 6 slices of white bread on one tray for about 5 minutes. The back left slices go dark, so her oven card says: back left is hottest, turn trays halfway.

21
00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:57.000
Keep the gas cylinder upright, in a ventilated place, away from the burners and out of children's reach.

22
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.040
Replace a hose that is cracked, hard or loose.

23
00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:10.140
To check for a leak, brush soapy water on the joints. Growing bubbles mean gas is escaping. Never test with a flame.

24
00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:14.680
Have the lit match ready at the burner before you turn the knob.

25
00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.870
Turn the cylinder off every evening.

26
00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:29.140
If you smell gas, do not light anything or touch switches. Turn off the valve if safe, open windows, get everyone out, and call a gas technician or the fire service.

27
00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:40.820
Your habits are your promise to customers. Wash your hands with soap for 40 to 60 seconds, and again after raw egg or fish, money, your phone or the toilet.

28
00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:47.710
Cover your hair, wear a clean apron, remove rings, and cover cuts with a waterproof plaster.

29
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:57.850
Do not produce for sale if you have diarrhoea or vomiting. Many food businesses wait until at least 48 hours after symptoms stop.

30
00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:07.720
Use the WHO Five Keys as your checklist. Clean with hot soapy water first, then sanitise. Keep raw food apart from finished snacks.

31
00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:14.850
Cook fillings until steaming hot, never leave them out for more than 2 hours, and use treated or boiled water.

32
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:28.860
To remember. Weigh everything. Store sealed and use the oldest first. Test raising agents. Know your real oven temperature and hot spots. And test gas joints with soapy water only.

33
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:40.860
Now practise. Set up your ingredient store and write a buying list with a 10% margin. Make your oven card. Then write your cleaning schedule and tape it where you will see it.

34
00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.150
Continue with the lessons below.
